    <section class="blocks-container glue-page glue-spacer-3-top">
      <div class="block">
          

<img
    class="banner-image"
    src="https://storage.googleapis.com/gweb-developer-goog-blog-assets/images/title-image.original.png"
    alt="title-image"
/>  <div class="inner-block-content rich-content">
    <p data-block-key="puy5s">I help lead Google&#x27;s <a href="https://github.com/google/skills">agent skills program</a>, currently with 175 agent skills that supplement community collections like <a href="https://github.com/spf13/go-skills">go-skills</a>. To keep that many skill files consistent, I wrote skill-lint, a standalone <a href="https://go.dev/?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026">Go</a> CLI that checks SKILL.md files and applies LLM-generated line edits before they ship. Even with skill-lint&#x27;s own unit and fuzz test suite, a small slice or line-number mistake inside its Markdown parsers or edit applier can sit dormant until an unusual file or edit range triggers a runtime panic in the linter.</p><p data-block-key="d005v">In Go, every slice or index operation that can panic at runtime compiles down to an explicit bounds-check trap in machine code, after the compiler&#x27;s optimizer strips out the checks it can already rule out. To hunt for crashes, I compiled key functions from my code to see which panic traps survived optimization, and <a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models/gemini-4-argon?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026">Gemini 4 Argon</a> translated the path to each trap into rules for the <a href="https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3">Z3 constraint solver</a>. An LLM-written solver model isn&#x27;t a sound proof system, but it gives the agent an independent way to reason about control flow and synthesize concrete Go reproducers, and every crash it finds is real because go test runs the offending bytes directly. That workflow caught all three bugs I planted in test parsers and turned up three latent bugs in skill-lint.</p><p data-block-key="9qn6o">In this post, I&#x27;ll walk through how the Gemini and Z3 pipeline works, why <a href="https://go.dev/security/fuzz/?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026">go test -fuzz</a> missed two of those crashes (and the one-line change that fixes it), and where this approach fits in a day-to-day Go workflow.</p><h3 data-block-key="jh6mn" id="how-the-solver-pipeline-works">How the solver pipeline works</h3><p data-block-key="53ed1">A <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/formal-verification">recent post from Anthropic</a> showed how formal verification catches latent bugs in C and C++ by having frontier AI models write machine-checked <a href="https://rocq-prover.org/">Rocq</a> proofs across <a href="https://github.com/google/oss-fuzz">OSS-Fuzz</a> libraries. Instead of sampling random inputs like a fuzzer, a formal tool works backward from each failure condition to solve for the exact input that triggers it. Full theorem proving is still too heavy for most day-to-day Go projects, so I wanted to see how much of that bug-hunting power an agent can get from an LLM and a constraint solver in about a minute per function.</p><p data-block-key="10q05">In C and C++, reading or writing past the end of a buffer corrupts memory. Go catches out-of-bounds access at runtime instead, compiling every slice or index operation it can&#x27;t prove safe into an explicit branch to <a href="https://github.com/golang/go/blob/go1.27.3/src/runtime/panic.go#L208">runtime.panicBounds</a> that panics, crashing the process unless recovered.</p><p data-block-key="15ss1">Before generating machine code, Go&#x27;s <a href="https://pkg.go.dev/cmd/compile/internal/ssa?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026">SSA</a> <a href="https://github.com/golang/go/blob/go1.27.3/src/cmd/compile/internal/ssa/prove.go">prove</a> pass already eliminates most of those checks by tracking slice lengths and loop variables. That optimization pass leaves behind only the <code>runtime.panicBounds</code> traps the compiler couldn&#x27;t rule out on its own, giving the solver a short list of candidate crash sites to target.</p><p data-block-key="8dlrc">I compiled each target with <code>GOOS=linux GOARCH=riscv64 go build -gcflags=&quot;-S -d=ssa/check_bce/debug=1&quot;</code> (<code>-S</code> prints the assembly listing and <code>ssa/check_bce/debug=1</code> logs every surviving bounds check) and passed both the Go source and the disassembly to the model. Looking at Go source alone, you can&#x27;t see which bounds checks the compiler already removed or where a register gets truncated to 8 bits; in the disassembly, both show up. I targeted <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RISC-V">RISC-V</a> because it&#x27;s the instruction set Anthropic&#x27;s post targets for its Rocq proofs, and Go cross-compiles to it out of the box.</p><p data-block-key="a9vcf">From there, Argon read the Go source and disassembly and returned a Z3 script modeling the path to each surviving trap, along with a patched function whenever it flagged a bug. Z3 then checked whether those constraints could be satisfied, and whenever it found a crashing input, <code>go test</code> replayed those bytes against the real function. Using an LLM saved me from translating assembly into solver constraints by hand. Argon&#x27;s translation dominated the runtime, at about a minute per function, while Z3 itself finished in milliseconds.</p>
</div>   


    
    <div class="inner-block-content">
        <div class="image-wrapper">
            
                <img
                    class="regular-image"
                    src="https://storage.googleapis.com/gweb-developer-goog-blog-assets/images/verification-harness.original.png"
                    alt="verification-harness"
                />
            
            
                
                    <div class="regular-image-description">
                        Go&#x27;s SSA compiler strips out provably safe bounds checks first. Gemini 4 Argon then models the remaining traps for Z3 to check.
                    </div>
                
            
        </div>
    </div>
  <div class="inner-block-content rich-content">
    <h3 data-block-key="161to" id="when-to-trust-the-solver"><b>When to trust the solver</b></h3><p data-block-key="4i4m4">Anthropic&#x27;s approach relies on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proof_assistant">interactive theorem proving</a>, where experts model machine semantics and Rocq checks LLM-generated proofs against those rules. In contrast, my workflow uses a constraint solver based on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satisfiability_modulo_theories">satisfiability modulo theories (SMT)</a>, relying on Argon to translate assembly directly into Z3 constraints. While a constraint solver doesn&#x27;t provide a complete correctness proof without a semantic model, it is highly effective for finding bugs. Z3&#x27;s <a href="https://microsoft.github.io/z3guide/docs/logic/basiccommands">check-sat</a> command returns sat when input constraints can be satisfied and <code>unsat</code> when they cannot.</p><p data-block-key="d3os6">A sat answer (&quot;definitely buggy&quot;) comes with concrete bytes aimed at the trap, and <code>go test</code> runs them against the real function. Once go test panics, you have a concrete bug reproducer regardless of whether Argon&#x27;s model was sound. Like a Bloom filter for bugs, an <code>unsat</code> answer only means &quot;maybe correct.&quot; Trading away a correctness proof is what makes the approach so practical: you get most of the bug-finding value of formal methods for a tiny fraction of the effort.</p><p data-block-key="cqkvf">Anthropic&#x27;s setup proved no-crash properties on whole binaries under a 64 MB budget; I checked individual functions on fresh 64-byte slices where <a href="https://go.dev/ref/spec?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026#Length_and_capacity">cap(buf) == len(buf)</a>, so any slice past <code>len(buf)</code> panics. (If you want the full-binary Rocq setup for Go, <a href="https://github.com/theorem-labs/oss-verify">theorem-labs/oss-verify</a> includes a <a href="https://go.dev/doc/go1.27?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026">Go 1.27</a> runtime overlay that makes the runtime single-threaded and deterministic.)</p><p data-block-key="15k4p">For the fuzzing baseline, I ran unseeded <a href="https://go.dev/security/fuzz/?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026">go test -fuzz</a> on Go 1.27.3 against each planted bug: one 3-second run and three 60-second runs, each with a fresh fuzz cache so a short time budget wouldn&#x27;t explain a miss.</p><h3 data-block-key="h0zkq" id="finding-a-planted-bug-in-assembly"><b>Finding a planted bug in assembly</b></h3><p data-block-key="36vj4">I started with three safe parsers to make sure the pipeline didn&#x27;t flag clean code. The compiler&#x27;s <code>prove</code> pass removed every bounds check in a TOML key parser on its own, and Z3 returned <code>unsat</code> on the remaining traps in an HTTP header scanner and a varint decoder.</p><p data-block-key="5vb17">Then I wrote three parsers with subtle panics. Here&#x27;s the first one, <code>ParseTwoChunkFrame</code>:</p>
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content code-block line-numbers">
    <pre><code class="language-go">func ParseTwoChunkFrame(buf []byte) ([]byte, []byte) {
	if len(buf) &lt; 4 || buf[0] != 0xA5 || buf[1] != 0x5A {
		return nil, nil
	}
	hdrLen, bodyLen := buf[2], buf[3]
	total := hdrLen + bodyLen
	if int(total)+4 &gt; len(buf) {
		return nil, nil
	}
	endHdr := 4 + int(hdrLen)
	endBody := endHdr + int(bodyLen)
	return buf[4:endHdr], buf[endHdr:endBody]
}</code></pre>
    <div class="language-block glue-font-weight-medium">
        Go
    </div>
