    </div>
    
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content rich-content">
    <p data-block-key="puy5s">In the RISC-V disassembly, the bug is much easier to see:</p>
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content code-block line-numbers">
    <pre><code class="language-plaintext">0x0060  ADD    X16, X9, X5                 ; X5 = hdrLen + bodyLen
0x0064  MOVBU  X5, X5                      ; TRUNCATE to uint8: X5 = X5 &amp; 0xff
0x0068  ADDI   $4, X5, X5                  ; X5 = int(total) + 4
0x006a  BLT    X11, X5, 0x00bc             ; guard: if len(buf) &lt; X5, return nil, nil
0x006e  MOV    X9, X11                     ; X11 = int(hdrLen)
0x0070  MOV    X16, X14                    ; X14 = int(bodyLen)
0x0072  ADD    X14, X11, X9                ; X9 = int(hdrLen) + int(bodyLen) (unmasked!)
0x0076  ADDI   $4, X11, X16                ; X16 = endHdr = 4 + int(hdrLen)
0x007a  BLTU   X12, X16, 0x00ec            ; if cap(buf) &lt; endHdr -&gt; runtime.panicBounds
...
0x0090  ADDI   $4, X9, X9                  ; X9 = endBody = 4 + int(hdrLen) + int(bodyLen)
0x0092  BLTU   X12, X9, 0x00e8             ; if cap(buf) &lt; endBody -&gt; runtime.panicBounds</code></pre>
    <div class="language-block glue-font-weight-medium">
        Plain text
    </div>
    <div class="copy-code-block">
        <span class="hidden">Copied</span>
        <button class="copy-clipboard">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="24px" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" width="24px" fill="#202124"><path d="M360-240q-33 0-56.5-23.5T280-320v-480q0-33 23.5-56.5T360-880h360q33 0 56.5 23.5T800-800v480q0 33-23.5 56.5T720-240H360Zm0-80h360v-480H360v480ZM200-80q-33 0-56.5-23.5T120-160v-560h80v560h440v80H200Zm160-240v-480 480Z"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    <div class="dark-mode-block">
        <button class="dark-mode-toggle">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="24px" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" width="24px" fill="#202124"><path d="M480-120q-150 0-255-105T120-480q0-150 105-255t255-105q14 0 27.5 1t26.5 3q-41 29-65.5 75.5T444-660q0 90 63 153t153 63q55 0 101-24.5t75-65.5q2 13 3 26.5t1 27.5q0 150-105 255T480-120Zm0-80q88 0 158-48.5T740-375q-20 5-40 8t-40 3q-123 0-209.5-86.5T364-660q0-20 3-40t8-40q-78 32-126.5 102T200-480q0 116 82 198t198 82Zm-10-270Z"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content rich-content">
    <p data-block-key="puy5s"><code>total := hdrLen + bodyLen</code> adds two <code>uint8</code> values, so <code>MOVBU</code> <a href="https://go.dev/ref/spec?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026#Integer_overflow">wraps the sum at 256</a> before the length guard checks it, while <code>endBody</code> adds the full 64-bit ints. Argon turned those instructions into a Z3 query asking whether a fresh slice of up to 64 bytes can pass the guard and still fail the <code>endBody</code> check at <code>0x0092</code>:</p>
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content code-block line-numbers">
    <pre><code class="language-python">import z3

s = z3.Solver()
length, capacity = z3.BitVecs(&quot;length capacity&quot;, 64)
b0, b1, hdrLen8, bodyLen8 = z3.BitVecs(&quot;b0 b1 hdrLen8 bodyLen8&quot;, 8)

wrapped_sum64 = z3.ZeroExt(56, hdrLen8 + bodyLen8) + z3.BitVecVal(4, 64)
end_hdr64 = z3.BitVecVal(4, 64) + z3.ZeroExt(56, hdrLen8)
end_body64 = end_hdr64 + z3.ZeroExt(56, bodyLen8)

s.add(z3.UGE(length, 4), z3.ULE(length, 64), capacity == length)
s.add(b0 == 0xA5, b1 == 0x5A)
s.add(z3.UGE(length, wrapped_sum64), z3.ULE(end_hdr64, capacity))
s.add(z3.UGT(end_body64, capacity))  # panic at 0x0092
assert s.check() == z3.sat
print(s.model())</code></pre>
    <div class="language-block glue-font-weight-medium">
        Python
    </div>
    <div class="copy-code-block">
        <span class="hidden">Copied</span>
        <button class="copy-clipboard">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="24px" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" width="24px" fill="#202124"><path d="M360-240q-33 0-56.5-23.5T280-320v-480q0-33 23.5-56.5T360-880h360q33 0 56.5 23.5T800-800v480q0 33-23.5 56.5T720-240H360Zm0-80h360v-480H360v480ZM200-80q-33 0-56.5-23.5T120-160v-560h80v560h440v80H200Zm160-240v-480 480Z"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    <div class="dark-mode-block">
        <button class="dark-mode-toggle">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="24px" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" width="24px" fill="#202124"><path d="M480-120q-150 0-255-105T120-480q0-150 105-255t255-105q14 0 27.5 1t26.5 3q-41 29-65.5 75.5T444-660q0 90 63 153t153 63q55 0 101-24.5t75-65.5q2 13 3 26.5t1 27.5q0 150-105 255T480-120Zm0-80q88 0 158-48.5T740-375q-20 5-40 8t-40 3q-123 0-209.5-86.5T364-660q0-20 3-40t8-40q-78 32-126.5 102T200-480q0 116 82 198t198 82Zm-10-270Z"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content rich-content">
    <p data-block-key="puy5s">Z3 returned a 59-byte counterexample with <code>hdrLen = 48</code> and <code>bodyLen = 236</code>: their <code>uint8</code> sum wraps from 284 down to 28 (so 28 + 4 ≤ 59 passes the guard), and <code>buf[endHdr:endBody]</code> then slices to index 288 and panics.</p><h3 data-block-key="snvol" id="why-go&#x27;s-fuzzer-missed-a-1-in-5-crash">Why Go&#x27;s fuzzer missed a 1-in-5 crash</h3><p data-block-key="2gif1">At 59 bytes, 19.4% of all possible <code>(hdrLen, bodyLen)</code> pairs trigger this crash. Yet a 3-second fuzz run missed it, and so did three 60-second runs.</p><p data-block-key="90p8l">Go checks a <a href="https://go.dev/ref/spec?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026#Slice_expressions">slice expression</a> like <code>buf[endHdr:endBody]</code> against the slice&#x27;s capacity, not its length, and Go&#x27;s fuzzer rarely passes a slice without spare capacity. In a 10-second probe of 386,741 fuzz calls, 89% of inputs were views into a shared ~100 MB buffer, and only 0.07% had <code>cap == len</code>. With that much spare capacity, endBody (at most 514) never exceeds <code>cap(buf)</code>, so the buggy parser returns bytes past the end of <code>buf</code> instead of panicking.</p><p data-block-key="4a43e">The second target, <code>ExtractQuotedToken</code>, hit the same blind spot when a trailing backslash pushed the index one byte past <code>len(buf)</code>. Z3 caught it immediately with the 2-byte input <code>[]byte{&#x27;&quot;&#x27;, &#x27;\\&#x27;}</code>, while the fuzzer absorbed the overrun in spare capacity.</p><p data-block-key="6miao">Clipping capacity to length with a full slice expression turns any slice past <code>len</code> into a panic:</p>
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content code-block line-numbers">
    <pre><code class="language-go">f.Fuzz(func(t *testing.T, data []byte) {
	data = data[:len(data):len(data)] // cap == len: slicing past the end now panics
	ParseTwoChunkFrame(data)
})</code></pre>
    <div class="language-block glue-font-weight-medium">
        Go
    </div>
    <div class="copy-code-block">
        <span class="hidden">Copied</span>
        <button class="copy-clipboard">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="24px" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" width="24px" fill="#202124"><path d="M360-240q-33 0-56.5-23.5T280-320v-480q0-33 23.5-56.5T360-880h360q33 0 56.5 23.5T800-800v480q0 33-23.5 56.5T720-240H360Zm0-80h360v-480H360v480ZM200-80q-33 0-56.5-23.5T120-160v-560h80v560h440v80H200Zm160-240v-480 480Z"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    <div class="dark-mode-block">
        <button class="dark-mode-toggle">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="24px" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" width="24px" fill="#202124"><path d="M480-120q-150 0-255-105T120-480q0-150 105-255t255-105q14 0 27.5 1t26.5 3q-41 29-65.5 75.5T444-660q0 90 63 153t153 63q55 0 101-24.5t75-65.5q2 13 3 26.5t1 27.5q0 150-105 255T480-120Zm0-80q88 0 158-48.5T740-375q-20 5-40 8t-40 3q-123 0-209.5-86.5T364-660q0-20 3-40t8-40q-78 32-126.5 102T200-480q0 116 82 198t198 82Zm-10-270Z"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content rich-content">
    <p data-block-key="puy5s">After that change, the fuzzer crashed both <code>ParseTwoChunkFrame</code> and <code>ExtractQuotedToken</code> in all three runs (median 0.28 s and 0.17 s). The third target, <code>ParseMagicPacket</code>, was a control because its signed <code>int8</code> length bug panics on a negative index regardless of capacity, and the fuzzer found it in every run either way. Fuzzing still can&#x27;t give you an <code>unsat</code> signal across the remaining paths, which is how I checked the three safe parsers and Argon&#x27;s patches.</p>
</div>   


    
    <div class="inner-block-content">
        <div class="image-wrapper">
            
                <img
                    class="regular-image"
                    src="https://storage.googleapis.com/gweb-developer-goog-blog-assets/images/parser-benchmark-table.original.png"
                    alt="parser-benchmark-table"
                />
            
            
                
                    <div class="regular-image-description">
                        Clipping slice capacity to length fixes the fuzzer&#x27;s blind spot on both overrun bugs, while Argon and Z3 catch all three crashes and check each patched parser.
                    </div>
                
            
        </div>
    </div>
  <div class="inner-block-content rich-content">
    <h3 data-block-key="1g4rz" id="running-it-on-skill-lint"><b>Running it on skill-lint</b></h3><p data-block-key="e7be7">Next, I pointed the pipeline at skill-lint itself. A whole Go CLI is too large to hand to a solver directly. Even a 10-line parser links 1,559 symbols from the Go runtime, and the 28 MB skill-lint binary has 32,321 symbols in total. Following <a href="https://www.destroyallsoftware.com/screencasts/catalog/functional-core-imperative-shell">Functional Core, Imperative Shell</a>, I left CLI flags, network calls, and file I/O to <code>go test -fuzz</code> and <code>go test -race</code>, and ran the solver only on pure functions and slice helpers extracted from file-reading code.</p>
</div>   


    
    <div class="inner-block-content">
        <div class="image-wrapper">
            
                <img
                    class="regular-image"
                    src="https://storage.googleapis.com/gweb-developer-goog-blog-assets/images/verification-rings.original.png"
                    alt="verification-rings"
                />
            
            
                
                    <div class="regular-image-description">
                        Pull parsing and slice math into pure functions for the solver, and leave file I/O, CLI flags, and network calls to standard fuzzing and race detection.
                    </div>
                
            
        </div>
    </div>
  <div class="inner-block-content rich-content">
    <p data-block-key="z0r5m">Of the five functions I tested, two scanners came back <code>unsat</code> (including a CRLF line splitter with six surviving bounds checks), and the other three had bugs. The sharpest was in <code>ApplyReplacementChunks</code>, which validates LLM-generated <code>{StartLine, EndLine}</code> edits against the initial line count and sorts them bottom-to-top so earlier edits don&#x27;t shift later line numbers:</p>
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content code-block line-numbers">
    <pre><code class="language-go">for _, c := range chunks {
	if c.StartLine &lt; 1 || c.EndLine &gt; len(lines) || c.StartLine &gt; c.EndLine {
		return nil
	}
}
sort.Slice(chunks, func(i, j int) bool {
	return chunks[i].StartLine &gt; chunks[j].StartLine
})
for _, c := range chunks {
	lines = append(lines[:c.StartLine-1],
		append(c.Replacement, lines[c.EndLine:]...)...)
}</code></pre>
    <div class="language-block glue-font-weight-medium">
        Go
    </div>
    <div class="copy-code-block">
        <span class="hidden">Copied</span>
        <button class="copy-clipboard">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="24px" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" width="24px" fill="#202124"><path d="M360-240q-33 0-56.5-23.5T280-320v-480q0-33 23.5-56.5T360-880h360q33 0 56.5 23.5T800-800v480q0 33-23.5 56.5T720-240H360Zm0-80h360v-480H360v480ZM200-80q-33 0-56.5-23.5T120-160v-560h80v560h440v80H200Zm160-240v-480 480Z"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    <div class="dark-mode-block">
        <button class="dark-mode-toggle">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" height="24px" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" width="24px" fill="#202124"><path d="M480-120q-150 0-255-105T120-480q0-150 105-255t255-105q14 0 27.5 1t26.5 3q-41 29-65.5 75.5T444-660q0 90 63 153t153 63q55 0 101-24.5t75-65.5q2 13 3 26.5t1 27.5q0 150-105 255T480-120Zm0-80q88 0 158-48.5T740-375q-20 5-40 8t-40 3q-123 0-209.5-86.5T364-660q0-20 3-40t8-40q-78 32-126.5 102T200-480q0 116 82 198t198 82Zm-10-270Z"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    
</div>  <div class="inner-block-content rich-content">
    <p data-block-key="z0r5m">Z3 immediately returned <code>sat</code> because sorting by <code>StartLine</code> doesn&#x27;t prevent two chunks from overlapping. In the extracted helper, which checks bounds once up front, replacing lines 3 and 4 of a 4-line file with one line shrinks <code>lines</code> to 3 elements, so a second chunk spanning lines 2 through 4 evaluates <code>lines[4:]</code> and panics with <code>slice bounds out of range [4:3]</code>. In the shipped <code>ApplyChunks</code>, a per-iteration bounds check turns that case into a misleading range error, while overlapping chunks that stay within <code>len(lines)</code> silently overwrite the earlier edit. Rejecting overlapping chunks after the sort fixes both.</p><p data-block-key="46q26">The other two bugs were similar accounting errors. In the local fixer, target line numbers were checked once per violation before the inner chunk loop applied deletions that shrank the slice (a latent panic if a fixer ever returns a second chunk after a deletion, though <code>DeleteLineFix</code> only returns one today). In the prompt packer, a truncation check reserved a flat 100 bytes for a ~70-byte wrapper plus file path, overshooting the 2 MB context budget in the shipped code when a path exceeds about 30 bytes and panicking in the extracted helper.</p><p data-block-key="2ql2u">When you check a function modularly like this, the target has to include its own entry-point guards (like the bounds check and sort in <code>ApplyReplacementChunks</code>). If an internal helper assumes preconditions that are only enforced by its caller or stated in a doc comment, Z3 will happily invent inputs that violate them. Because each extracted helper included its entry guards, false positives weren&#x27;t an issue: across all six buggy functions in my tests, the first <code>sat</code> assignment Z3 returned triggered a real panic in <code>go test</code> without needing to filter out non-crashing candidates or loop back into the solver.</p><p data-block-key="5bcqg">Once I pulled these helpers away from file I/O, a 3-second fuzz run caught all three bugs too. To make sure Argon wasn&#x27;t leaning on source comments in my test files, I also reran the skill-lint targets with all comments stripped; Argon and Z3 still caught all three bugs and returned <code>unsat</code> on the two scanners and all three patches.</p><h3 data-block-key="z49i2" id="where-this-fits-in-a-go-workflow"><b>Where this fits in a Go workflow</b></h3><p data-block-key="9an8m">Staying entirely inside the target language makes a whole class of bugs hard for an agent to see. When an LLM reviews Go source directly, a line like <code>if int(total)+4 &gt; len(buf)</code> looks like a textbook bounds check, and when it runs <code>go test -fuzz</code>, target-language runtime details like spare slice capacity or multi-step precondition checks can hide the crash indefinitely. Asking the agent to translate Go and assembly into a constraint solver forces a change of representation that strips away surface syntax and makes every bit width and slice bound explicit, while <code>go test</code> turns any solver hit back into a concrete Go reproducer.</p><p data-block-key="6cg4o">For most Go code, fuzzing pure helpers with slice capacity clipped to length will catch bounds bugs in seconds. A solver is worth the extra minute on the traps that survive both the compiler&#x27;s range prover and a quick fuzz run, especially in parsers and editors that compute offsets from multi-field headers or line ranges.</p><p data-block-key="86gas">Single-threaded solver models don&#x27;t see data races or deadlocks, so concurrent code still belongs with the <a href="https://go.dev/doc/articles/race_detector?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026">race detector</a>, <a href="https://go.dev/pkg/testing/synctest?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026">deterministic concurrency testing</a>, and Go 1.27&#x27;s <a href="https://go.dev/doc/go1.27?utm_team=DSS+DevRel&amp;utm_campaign=default&amp;utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_content=b583878026#goroutineleak-profiles">goroutine leak profiles</a>.</p><p data-block-key="7d1g">If you&#x27;re experimenting with solvers or coding agents on Go, let&#x27;s compare notes on <a href="https://x.com/kweinmeister">X</a> or <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/karlweinmeister/">LinkedIn</a>.</p>
</div> 
      </div>
    </section>
    

